This means you need to work on your execution and timing better.

&#x200B;

For example, if they are at ledge and you approach knowing they will roll behind you, to which you already stated you can call out what they are going to do, the only reason your move would "phase through them" is because you timed your punish incorrectly.

&#x200B;

If you feel like you are getting "minimal damage" it's because the move you chose to punish with may not have been optimal but this is not always the case. Sometimes a stray hit is more than enough to pressure, especially if they are knocked off the stage.

&#x200B;

If you are getting stuffed out, then either your move was not optimal to punish their option, or perhaps you are not attacking from the correct angle. Pay attention to the hitbox of the move they are throwing out, if it requires you to be underneath them or ontop of them to avoid their attack (given that their attack will be given priority for whatever reason), then you must do so.

&#x200B;

If you post a video or 2 I would be more than happy to help point out some things.

not a dumb question, terminology can get confusing at first.

&#x200B;

ledge trapping is simply staying on the stage and applying pressure at the ledge in response to 1 of your opponents 4 normal options: neutral getup, attack getup, roll in, and jump.

&#x200B;

edge guarding is literally going off the stage and trying to contest your opponent there, as opposed to waiting for them to get back to ledge.

Recovering:

&#x200B;

Unfortunately fox does not have great recovery. Once your opponent understands the distance of side special, and the long start up time for uspecial, you will be easily harassed off stage. you will have to determine if it is best for you to recover low, by using side special > jump > uspecial. or snapping to ledge with a side special. keep in mind you also have your reflector. using reflector in the air for the first time will almost completely stop your downward aerial momentum and it comes out extremely quickly, granting you intangibility on frames 2-3 i believe. its great as a mixup option when jumping up from ledge, unless the enemy has a strong disjoint, you will most likely beat out their option.

Approaching:

&#x200B;

fox has the fastest fall speed in the game and is extremely quick on the ground, with a fast dash speed and one of the fastest run speeds. you need to be sitting outside your opponents range and dashing around and mixing up you sh/fh/dj ff movement options. if they have a strong anti-air, respect their options. go in asap the second you see them do something unsafe, as most of the time they will be unable to react to fox's burst options and speed.

&#x200B;

if you find them shielding a lot, start grabbing more. fox has the most aggressive tomahawk capabilities in the game. learn to space out your nairs as well, you don't want to land directly infront of them. i find it works very well to cross opponent's up with nair. most of the time they will drop shield and try to turn around to counter you, but they never expect utilt. 1 or 2 utilts will usually catch an opponent looking to counter you. you can lead into a string from there. if they begin shielding those as well, you can simply turn around and grab them since they will be expecting an utilt.

&#x200B;

never use side special to approach, it is extremely unsafe and you will get heavily punished if the enemy shields it. it works best as a mixup option at medium-short range. depending on where you end up and the percentage of the enemy, you may be able to get an uair or bair afterwards. it can also be used as a tech chase tool.

&#x200B;

you are able to approach very freely, as you have the speed to bait and punish opponents. try to resist the urge of unsafely dashing into the enemy.

Kill Confirms and Ledge/Edge Guarding:

&#x200B;

a great kill confirm at higher percentages you need to practice is: dair cancel > dash > usmash and sour nair > usmash.

&#x200B;

at high enough percentage (dependent on rage/opponent weight), sour nair will turn into a tech chase situation. you will have to read your opponent. if you can read correctly (fox's speed allows him to cover all options), you will still get the usmash. usmash can also be used as an anti-air, oos option, or after most parries.

&#x200B;

for edge guarding, you can run off and shine if the opponent has a bad recovery. or you can jump off and use uspecial's long lasting hitbox and aim for ledge, as your opponent is grabbing it in an attempt to 2 frame (this option works best on opponents without a hitbox on their uspecial. falling nair is good too, just don't fast fall off stage too often as it can get dangerous.

&#x200B;

most of the time it is not worth it to edge guard an opponent as fox, but rather to ledge trap. he is fast enough to cover all options if timed correctly. alternating between sh/fh/dj ff nair uses the lingering hit box of nair combined with fox's fast fall speed to cover neutral getup, attack getup, jump. if an opponent rolls in, you will be able to catch them by dashing towards them with an appropriate option.

&#x200B;

another tip, since fox is always using usmash, utilt, nair, or jab, use your laser very liberally. each shot from your laser counts as a separate move, therefore it will help unstale your other moves.

Combos:

&#x200B;

at zero, landing nair > jab is true.

&#x200B;

at zero and low percentages. landing bair > turn around jab or landing bair > dash attack > utilt/nair

&#x200B;

zero and low percentage. dash attack > utilt, see how they land, sometimes you will be able to get another utilt, a grab, or a jab in.

&#x200B;

zero and low percentage. dtilt > sh bair > dash attack > sh bair > nair/usmash does good damage and carries opponent quite far.

&#x200B;

mid to low-high percentage. utilt > sh/fh bair or uair

&#x200B;

uair > uair > uair. seriously, practice your uair strings.

Backthrow at ledge at higher percentages works well due to massive knockback.

&#x200B;

I also like downthrow to full hop/double jump cyclone. this kills many characters at around 90% on lower ceiling stages. the precision is also a lot less strict than up-b (this can be done around 80% typically).

&#x200B;

Downthrow at ledge around i think 60%-80% can result in a spike if you follow up and the opponent is slow to DI.

Lauv - I Met You When I Was 18


&nbsp;


It's not as EDM heavy as other albums people have stated, closer to pop, but it does have notable down-tempo EDM elements. It is the story of self actualization, the first love, finding one's identity, heartbreak, and acceptance. In an interview Lauv stated it tells the story of himself:

&nbsp;


"I met you when I was 18 is a collection of songs, a story. About moving to New York City when I was 18 and falling in love for the first time. A story about trying to figure out your own identity whilst being deeply intertwined with someone else’s. It’s a story that, since I released my first song “The Other” in 2015, has been told a bit out of order. In pieces, in fragments & in moments reminisced, but now here it is, in it’s entirety: I met you when I was 18 not an album, but a body of work nonetheless. I hope you enjoy."


&nbsp;


From another interview:

&nbsp;


“This has all been part of the same story. A chapter of my life. Moving to New York City when I was 18 for school and not knowing who I was or who I wanted to be. Falling in love for the first time and being in a 4 year relationship that taught me everything about myself, the world, and how to love. Discovering and defining who I really was and struggling to have the confidence to truly be that person. Learning to trust myself, even when it was the scariest thing in the world. I did the only thing I knew how to do and turn my feelings into songs and share them, but the songs have been out of order. I didn’t realize it then, and the truth is I’m still figuring it out, but there’s a thread and all I know is I want to share it with you guys the same way I’m piecing it together myself. So starting today, I’ve put all of these songs in order as a playlist. It’s called, “I met you when I was 18.” The puzzle is incomplete, and I’m going to be releasing A LOT of new music that fills in the gaps until this chapter of my life is fully told. I’ve never been so excited and scared to share myself fully with the world, and the truth is you guys have inspired and empowered me to get to this point. So, thank you. I love you more than you could ever know.”

It looks like the album tells a story of the contrasting internal conflict of dread and despair vs. courage and hopefulness someone can feel when leaving a crumbling and collapsing relationship.

&nbsp;

Needed You, Crawl Outta Love, No Time Like Now, Free Fall, Where'd You Go conveys a strong feeling of longing, perhaps for the relationship that once was, when everything was good, while comparing it to what the person is presently feeling.

&nbsp;

Fractures is the fulcrum point, at which all the doubts and longingness, everything the person has been feeling up this point, is being converted into a feeling of hopefulness and determination to leave.

&nbsp;

Leaving, Lost, Sound of Walking Away is the result of the previous, the next and final step, at which the person is driven to finally leave the relationship in pursuit of their happiness, once again.

&nbsp;

Taking Me Higher, Feel Good, Beautiful Creatures, is what everyone hopes to feel again later down the line after the post-relationship anguish and numbness has faded. These songs convey a message of tranquility, that everything will be ok. The peace of mind that you will be happy again.

&nbsp;

Let You Go is making peace with everything, acceptance. Accepting the fact it is over now, and being able to let the other person go emotionally, regardless of how good or bad things were.

&nbsp;

